1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,109
<font color="#FFFFFF">Organizations increasingly want to pool their data
to build better machine learning models,</font>

2
00:00:06,189 --> 00:00:11,798
<font color="#FFFFFF">but privacy policies and intellectual property laws
often make raw data sharing impossible.</font>

3
00:00:11,878 --> 00:00:17,613
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces a privacy-preserving
framework that uses autoencoders to turn sensitive</font>

4
00:00:17,693 --> 00:00:20,926
<font color="#FFFFFF">tabular data into obfuscated latent-space embeddings.</font>

5
00:00:21,006 --> 00:00:23,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of exchanging raw features,</font>

6
00:00:23,194 --> 00:00:26,928
<font color="#FFFFFF">collaborating peers share only these encoded
representations,</font>

7
00:00:27,008 --> 00:00:30,054
<font color="#FFFFFF">then join them to train a shared downstream model.</font>

8
00:00:30,134 --> 00:00:34,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across three public benchmarks, the encoded data
preserves predictive power,</font>

9
00:00:34,886 --> 00:00:41,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">with performance dropping by less than ten
percentage points while the original features stay hidden.</font>

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,939
<font color="#FFFFFF">A common real-world situation is two companies that
each hold a different set of variables</font>

11
00:00:47,019 --> 00:00:48,778
<font color="#FFFFFF">about the same group of users.</font>

12
00:00:48,858 --> 00:00:51,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">If they could combine their features,</font>

13
00:00:51,127 --> 00:00:56,566
<font color="#FFFFFF">both could predict a shared target variable far
more accurately and make better decisions.</font>

14
00:00:56,646 --> 00:00:58,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the data is sensitive,</font>

15
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:03,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">so privacy policies and intellectual property laws
forbid handing over the raw features,</font>

16
00:01:03,636 --> 00:01:07,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">even when the communication channel between them is
secure.</font>

17
00:01:07,254 --> 00:01:12,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">The collaboration is cancelled, and the potential
boost in model performance is lost.</font>

18
00:01:12,466 --> 00:01:17,598
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core problem this paper tackles is how to let
such peers share their information,</font>

19
00:01:17,678 --> 00:01:20,971
<font color="#FFFFFF">and keep the predictive power of the original features,</font>

20
00:01:21,051 --> 00:01:23,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">without ever exposing the raw sensitive data.</font>

21
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:28,609
<font color="#FFFFFF">Academia and industry have built several
privacy-preserving strategies,</font>

22
00:01:28,689 --> 00:01:29,899
<font color="#FFFFFF">but each has limits.</font>

23
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:35,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">Encryption approaches like homomorphic encryption
offer strong security yet are hard to</font>

24
00:01:35,591 --> 00:01:39,704
<font color="#FFFFFF">deploy in real settings because of their technology
requirements.</font>

25
00:01:39,784 --> 00:01:43,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">Federated learning decentralizes training across devices,</font>

26
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:47,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it assumes every peer holds the same kind of
information,</font>

27
00:01:47,395 --> 00:01:51,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">so it cannot handle two peers that contribute
different features.</font>

28
00:01:51,587 --> 00:01:55,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">Differential privacy masks individual values by
adding noise,</font>

29
00:01:55,522 --> 00:01:58,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">which can significantly reduce data utility.</font>

30
00:01:58,359 --> 00:02:03,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">Linear dimensionality reduction such as Principal
Component Analysis obfuscates features</font>

31
00:02:04,035 --> 00:02:06,987
<font color="#FFFFFF">but may lose important nonlinear relationships.</font>

32
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:12,663
<font color="#FFFFFF">These gaps motivate a new approach that uses deep
representation learning to encode data</font>

33
00:02:12,743 --> 00:02:15,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">while keeping its predictive structure intact.</font>

34
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:19,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

35
00:02:19,390 --> 00:02:22,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

36
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:26,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">it reviews existing privacy-preserving machine
learning approaches to expose their limitations</font>

37
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:29,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the room for improvement.</font>

38
00:02:29,530 --> 00:02:32,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it proposes a concrete framework in which
each peer trains an autoencoder,</font>

39
00:02:32,910 --> 00:02:36,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">shares only the latent-space representation of its data,</font>

40
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:39,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">and then joins these embeddings to train a shared
supervised model.</font>

41
00:02:39,670 --> 00:02:42,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

42
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:46,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">it validates the framework on three public datasets
spanning regression and classification,</font>

43
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:49,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">and across five experimental scenarios,</font>

44
00:02:49,810 --> 00:02:53,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">ranging from a raw-data baseline to non-naive
multitask autoencoders that also predict</font>

45
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:56,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">the target variable during encoding.</font>

46
00:02:56,870 --> 00:03:01,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">The framework inserts one extra step into the usual
data-sharing pipeline.</font>

47
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:03,939
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of merging raw datasets,</font>

48
00:03:04,019 --> 00:03:10,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">each peer first passes its features through an
autoencoder to obtain a latent-space representation,</font>

49
00:03:10,695 --> 00:03:14,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">an obfuscated version of the data ready to be shared.</font>

50
00:03:14,269 --> 00:03:20,259
<font color="#FFFFFF">The peers then join these representations using a
shared observation ID and train a single</font>

51
00:03:20,339 --> 00:03:25,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">supervised model on the combined embeddings to
predict the same target.</font>

52
00:03:25,127 --> 00:03:30,779
<font color="#FFFFFF">The autoencoder is fixed across all experiments so
conclusions reflect the framework,</font>

53
00:03:30,859 --> 00:03:37,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">not network tuning: the encoder has four layers
going from N input features through hidden sizes 128,</font>

54
00:03:37,671 --> 00:03:43,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">64, and 40 down to an embedding of size M, and the
decoder mirrors it back up to N.</font>

55
00:03:43,268 --> 00:03:46,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every layer uses ReLU, the loss is Mean Absolute Error,</font>

56
00:03:46,977 --> 00:03:51,483
<font color="#FFFFFF">and training uses the Adam optimizer with a
learning rate of 0.0001.</font>

57
00:03:51,563 --> 00:03:53,304
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the non-naive scenarios,</font>

58
00:03:53,384 --> 00:03:59,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">the autoencoder becomes a multitask network that
also predicts the objective variable,</font>

59
00:03:59,184 --> 00:04:03,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the encoding is guided by the downstream
prediction task.</font>

60
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:07,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test the framework under realistic and varied
conditions,</font>

61
00:04:07,618 --> 00:04:10,263
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors chose three public datasets.</font>

62
00:04:10,343 --> 00:04:15,509
<font color="#FFFFFF">House Pricing has about twenty-one thousand
observations and twelve features,</font>

63
00:04:15,589 --> 00:04:19,734
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a regression target predicting house price in
US dollars.</font>

64
00:04:19,814 --> 00:04:25,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">MNIST Numbers has thirty-five thousand observations
and seven hundred eighty-four features,</font>

65
00:04:26,014 --> 00:04:30,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">treated as tabular data for a ten-class digit
classification task.</font>

66
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:36,699
<font color="#FFFFFF">Buzz in Social Media has over eighty-seven thousand
observations and seventy-seven features,</font>

67
00:04:36,779 --> 00:04:38,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">again a regression task.</font>

68
00:04:38,414 --> 00:04:43,784
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together they cover regression and classification,
small and large feature sets,</font>

69
00:04:43,864 --> 00:04:45,692
<font color="#FFFFFF">and different feature types,</font>

70
00:04:45,772 --> 00:04:50,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">letting the authors probe how robust and scalable
the encoding approach is.</font>

71
00:04:50,882 --> 00:04:56,389
<font color="#FFFFFF">All experiments ran on a modest setup: a two-core
Intel Xeon CPU at 2.3 gigahertz,</font>

72
00:04:56,469 --> 00:05:02,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">an Nvidia K80 or T4 GPU with twelve gigabytes of
memory, and twelve gigabytes of RAM.</font>

73
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:06,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">The central finding is that sharing encoded
representations instead of raw features barely</font>

74
00:05:06,357 --> 00:05:10,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">hurts predictive performance.</font>

75
00:05:10,154 --> 00:05:13,871
<font color="#FFFFFF">On House Pricing,</font>

76
00:05:13,951 --> 00:05:17,669
<font color="#FFFFFF">the raw-data baseline reaches a test R-squared of
about ninety percent,</font>

77
00:05:17,749 --> 00:05:21,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the two-peer scenario using individual
autoencoders still reaches about eighty-nine percent,</font>

78
00:05:21,546 --> 00:05:25,263
<font color="#FFFFFF">an almost negligible drop despite the data being
obfuscated.</font>

79
00:05:25,343 --> 00:05:29,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Buzz in Social Media,</font>

80
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:32,857
<font color="#FFFFFF">the baseline R-squared is around ninety-six percent
and the encoded scenarios stay in</font>

81
00:05:32,937 --> 00:05:36,654
<font color="#FFFFFF">the high eighties to low nineties.</font>

82
00:05:36,734 --> 00:05:40,451
<font color="#FFFFFF">For MNIST digit classification,</font>

83
00:05:40,531 --> 00:05:44,249
<font color="#FFFFFF">test accuracy moves from ninety-two percent at
baseline down to the mid-eighties under encoding.</font>

84
00:05:44,329 --> 00:05:48,046
<font color="#FFFFFF">Importantly, the non-naive multitask scenarios,</font>

85
00:05:48,126 --> 00:05:51,843
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the autoencoder is guided by the target
variable, recover much of this gap,</font>

86
00:05:51,923 --> 00:05:55,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that shaping the latent space with the
downstream task improves results.</font>

87
00:05:56,020 --> 00:06:02,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments are organized as an ablation across
five scenarios that isolate each design decision.</font>

88
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:06,771
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scenario zero is the raw-data baseline with no
privacy protection.</font>

89
00:06:06,851 --> 00:06:11,765
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scenario one uses a single shared autoencoder to
encode one combined dataset.</font>

90
00:06:11,845 --> 00:06:15,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scenario two simulates two peers, each with its own
autoencoder,</font>

91
00:06:15,996 --> 00:06:18,056
<font color="#FFFFFF">whose embeddings are then joined.</font>

92
00:06:18,136 --> 00:06:23,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scenarios three and four repeat one and two but
make the autoencoder non-naive: it becomes</font>

93
00:06:23,973 --> 00:06:28,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">a multitask network that also predicts the
objective variable during encoding.</font>

94
00:06:29,031 --> 00:06:34,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">The clear pattern is that the non-naive variants
beat their naive counterparts.</font>

95
00:06:34,155 --> 00:06:35,632
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Buzz in Social Media,</font>

96
00:06:35,712 --> 00:06:41,274
<font color="#FFFFFF">the test R-squared rises from about ninety-one and
a half percent with the naive shared</font>

97
00:06:41,354 --> 00:06:45,684
<font color="#FFFFFF">autoencoder to about ninety-four percent with the
non-naive version,</font>

98
00:06:45,764 --> 00:06:50,159
<font color="#FFFFFF">and on MNIST accuracy climbs from eighty-eight to
ninety-one percent.</font>

99
00:06:50,239 --> 00:06:54,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">Guiding the latent space with the target variable
clearly helps.</font>

100
00:06:54,690 --> 00:06:58,206
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

101
00:06:58,286 --> 00:07:01,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across all use cases,</font>

102
00:07:01,882 --> 00:07:05,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">replacing raw features with shared latent
representations dropped downstream performance</font>

103
00:07:05,478 --> 00:07:08,995
<font color="#FFFFFF">by less than ten percentage points,</font>

104
00:07:09,075 --> 00:07:12,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the reconstruction error of the autoencoder
ranged from five to eleven percent.</font>

105
00:07:12,671 --> 00:07:16,187
<font color="#FFFFFF">On House Pricing,</font>

106
00:07:16,267 --> 00:07:19,783
<font color="#FFFFFF">test R-squared fell only from ninety point two nine
percent at baseline to eighty-nine</font>

107
00:07:19,863 --> 00:07:23,379
<font color="#FFFFFF">point three three percent with two individual
autoencoders.</font>

108
00:07:23,459 --> 00:07:26,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Buzz in Social Media,</font>

109
00:07:27,055 --> 00:07:30,572
<font color="#FFFFFF">the non-naive shared autoencoder reached
ninety-four percent R-squared against a ninety-six percent baseline.</font>

110
00:07:30,652 --> 00:07:34,168
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the shared autoencoders correctly estimated
between ninety-six and ninety-eight percent</font>

111
00:07:34,248 --> 00:07:37,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">of observations per feature,</font>

112
00:07:37,844 --> 00:07:41,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming that the embeddings retain the core
structure of the data.</font>

113
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:45,681
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple: instead of exchanging raw
sensitive data,</font>

114
00:07:45,761 --> 00:07:51,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">collaborating organizations can share the
latent-space embeddings produced by autoencoders.</font>

115
00:07:51,390 --> 00:07:56,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">This keeps the original features private yet
preserves most of the predictive power,</font>

116
00:07:56,586 --> 00:08:01,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">with performance dropping by less than ten
percentage points across three benchmarks.</font>

117
00:08:01,844 --> 00:08:06,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">Making the autoencoder aware of the downstream task
narrows the gap even further.</font>

118
00:08:06,855 --> 00:08:12,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors point to custom per-dataset
autoencoders and formal measures of privacy strength</font>

119
00:08:12,546 --> 00:08:16,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">as the next steps toward deploying this in real
organizational settings.</font>
